{"v":1,"accession":"PRJNA1476348","title":"QS-PN","description":"Nitritation, the conversion of ammonia to nitrite without further oxidation, offers an energy-efficient route for nitrogen removal, and its application is limited by the difficulty of selectively suppressing nitrite-oxidizing bacteria (NOB). The underlying biological mechanisms that enable such suppression remain poorly understood. Here we show that quorum sensing (QS), a cell-cell communication system, enables nitritation by regulating NOB behaviour. Using multi-omics and single-cell Raman spectroscopy, we demonstrate that QS signaling induces the overexpression of nirB in the dominant NOB genus Nitrospira, triggering an altruistic nitrite reduction causing self-inactivation. In contrast, ammonia-oxidizing bacteria refrain from this altruistic metabolism, gaining a decisive competitive advantage and directing nitrification flux toward nitritation. QS manipulation confirms that active QS is required to maintain nitritation, and single-cell analysis reveals QS drives stress-tolerant Nitrospira cell into a susceptible state, markedly reducing survival. These findings uncover an unknown social behaviour in nitrifier community and offer new insights for nitritation stabilization.","center":"Chinese Academy of Sciences","data_type":"Raw sequence reads","submission_date":"2026/06/08 00:00","sample_cols":["accession","title","tier","context","collection_date","collection_year","country","region","place_id","place","location_status","lat","lon","submitted_location","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","eligibility_reason"],"samples":[["SAMN60711202","Nitrospira strain","probable_urban","municipal_wastewater","2026-02-25",2026,"CN","Beijing",1816670,"Beijing","verified",39.91,116.39,"China: Beijing","DSMZ commerical bacteria inoculum",null,null,null,"city-level location without sample-site confirmation"],["SAMN60711203","16s-FNA_0h-1","probable_urban","municipal_wastewater","2025-